<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tantamani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 664–656 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tanutamun · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tantamani was the last king of the Kushite 25th Dynasty to rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a> in 664 BCE, he briefly reconquered Memphis, but the Assyrian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> drove him south and sacked Thebes in 663 BCE. He continued to rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napata">Napata</a> and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-kurru">El-Kurru</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tantamani, also known as Tanutamun or Tanwetamani, ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and was the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-fifth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fifth Dynasty of Egypt</a>. His royal name was Bakare, meaning Glorious is the Ba and Ka of Re. Egyptological literature has sometimes made him a son of Shebitku, but Assyrian records call him a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabaka">Shabaka</a> and his mother Qalhata a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>, and it is now more common to regard him as Shabaka's son and Taharqa's nephew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> had appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-i">Necho I</a> as king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and left, Tantamani marched down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> hoping to restore his family to the throne. He reoccupied all of Egypt, including Memphis, and Necho I and the Assyrians' representative were killed. In 663 BCE Ashurbanipal returned in force. Together with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a>, which included Carian mercenaries, the Assyrians fought a pitched battle north of Memphis, near the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, and Tantamani was defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>.</p><p>Forty days after the battle the Assyrian army reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Tantamani had already left for Kipkipi, a place of uncertain location that might be Kom Ombo. Thebes was conquered and heavily plundered, its inhabitants deported, and Assyrian booty included two electrum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> weighing 2,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, about 75.5 tons. Egyptian sources do not mention the sack, which is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s annals, and the Book of Nahum refers to it. His authority was still recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> until his eighth year, 656 BCE, when the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> took Thebes peacefully and united all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, beginning the Twenty-sixth Dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Afterwards Tantamani ruled only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. He was succeeded by Atlanersa, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>, and was buried in the family cemetery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-kurru">El-Kurru</a>. His tomb lay below a pyramid, now vanished, and its entrance and chambers survive, decorated with mural paintings. In 2003 the archaeologist Charles Bonnet found a statue of Tantamani at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a>, now called Doukki Gel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tantamani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Esarhaddon took Memphis in 671 BCE, and after Taharqa's attempts to recover Egypt, Ashurbanipal's army sacked Thebes in 663 BCE, ending the rule of <b>Tantamani</b> in Egypt and forcing a retreat to Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2003 Bonnet’s team found near Kerma a cache of monumental black granite statues of the pharaohs of the Twenty-fifth Dynasty, among them Taharqa and <b>Tanutamun</b>, now shown in the Kerma Museum.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
